Working of partition-wise data storage
 - Range partitioning on Customer_ID: p0 = IDs below 1004, p1 = 1004 to 1006, p2 = 1007 and
   above.
 - On INSERT, MySQL applies the rule to Customer_ID and stores the row in the matching partition
   automatically.
 - How partitioning improves data management:
 - Faster queries: a query on a range of Customer_ID touches only the matching partition (partition
   pruning).
 - Easy maintenance: a partition can be backed up, repaired, optimised or dropped without touching
   the others (ALTER TABLE Customer DROP PARTITION p0).
 - Large tables become manageable: old customers can be archived by partition.
 - Better performance and availability: smaller indexes, and work on one partition does not block
   others.
 - Partitioning by city: use LIST COLUMNS (City) with partitions such as ('Pune','Nashik'), or
   HASH(Customer_ID) PARTITIONS 4 for even spread.
Conclusion: partitioning splits one big table into smaller physical parts by a rule on a column. The
user still writes normal queries on Customer, MySQL decides which partition stores each row, and
queries that name a partition (or filter on Customer_ID) read only that part.


