Q. 7. Explain the Star Schema with a suitable example of an e-commerce or college
database. Identify the fact table and any three dimension tables.
Definition
A star schema is the simplest data warehouse design. It has one central fact table that stores
measurable values (quantity, amount) and foreign keys, surrounded by dimension tables that
describe the facts (who, what, when). Drawn out, the fact table is in the centre and the dimensions
around it, like a star.

Example: E-commerce database
Fact table: Fact_Orders. Dimension tables: Dim_Product, Dim_Customer, Dim_Date.
Star schema diagram:
      [    Dim_Product     ]       [             Dim_Date        ]
              \                             /
                +------------------+
                |   Fact_Orders    |
                +------------------+
                         |
                    [   Dim_Customer         ]

Fact table: Fact_Orders
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Order_ID                      INT (PK)                 Unique id of each order line
  Product_ID                    INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Product
  Customer_ID                   INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Customer
  Date_ID                       INT (FK)                 Links to Dim_Date
  Quantity                      INT                      Measure: units ordered
  Order_Amount                  DECIMAL(10,2)            Measure: order value

Dimension table: Dim_Product
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Product_ID                    INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Product_Name                  VARCHAR(30)              Product name
  Category                      VARCHAR(20)              Category
  Brand                         VARCHAR(20)              Brand

Sample rows:
  +------------+--------------+-------------+---------+
  | Product_ID | Product_Name | Category    | Brand   |
  +------------+--------------+-------------+---------+
  | 1          | Smartphone   | Electronics | Samsung |
  | 2          | Jeans        | Clothing    | Levis   |
  | 3          | Novel        | Books       | Penguin |
  +------------+--------------+-------------+---------+

Dimension table: Dim_Customer
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Customer_ID                   INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Customer_Name                 VARCHAR(30)              Customer name
  City                          VARCHAR(20)              City
  State                         VARCHAR(20)              State

Sample rows:
  +-------------+---------------+--------+-------------+
  | Customer_ID | Customer_Name | City   | State       |
  +-------------+---------------+--------+-------------+
  | 1           | Ravi Patil    | Pune   | Maharashtra |
  | 2           | Priya Shah    | Mumbai | Maharashtra |
  | 3           | Karan Singh   | Delhi | Delhi        |
  +-------------+---------------+--------+-------------+

Dimension table: Dim_Date
  Column                        Type                     Meaning
  Date_ID                       INT (PK)                 Surrogate key
  Full_Date                     DATE                     Calendar date
  Month                         VARCHAR(10)              Month name
  Year                          INT                      Year

Sample rows:
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+
  | Date_ID | Full_Date | Month      | Year |
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+
  | 1       | 2026-08-05 | August    | 2026 |
  | 2       | 2026-09-10 | September | 2026 |
  | 3       | 2026-10-12 | October   | 2026 |
  +---------+------------+-----------+------+

Why star schema is useful
 - Simple design, easy for business users to understand.
 - Fast queries because only one join per dimension is needed.
 - Works well with OLAP operations (roll-up, drill-down, slice and dice).
 - Dimension tables can be reused across many fact tables.
Conclusion: in the E-commerce star schema, Fact_Orders is the fact table and Dim_Product,
Dim_Customer, Dim_Date are the dimension tables joined to it by foreign keys.
Sample fact rows: (1, Product 1, Customer 1, Date 1, 1, 18000.00), (2, Product 2, Customer 2, Date 2,
2, 3200.00), (3, Product 3, Customer 3, Date 3, 3, 900.00).


